/* V86 — the cut.

   The owner's first complaint was "too many monsters, which makes the game uninteresting", and the
   density of *systems* is a large part of that. This game has twenty-odd mechanics competing for a
   player's attention across a three-minute run, and several of them were built to solve problems
   that later parts solved better. Carrying them costs the player attention and costs every future
   change a compatibility question.

   Switched off at the constant rather than deleted, which is the same choice V83 made with V21's
   heat and for the same reasons: the parts being disabled contain other things that are not being
   disabled, the reasoning in their headers is the record of why the game is shaped as it is, and a
   measurement that disagrees with any of this should cost one line to answer rather than a revert.

   ---- 1. Mines ----

   A do-not-touch object in a game whose only verb is touch, on a touchscreen, where V71's own header
   admits the ergonomic risk of "a mis-tap on a phone with a thumb in the way". By its own three
   self-imposed limits it cannot threaten the reactor, cannot cost a life, and must never share a
   formation with a sealed hostile — so it is a pure punishment with no threat attached, and what it
   actually teaches is **hesitation**, which is the worst thing to teach in a game whose feel depends
   on confident fast input.

   Its stated job, making the player look before pressing, is done better by V65's sealed hostile,
   which asks for a positive action instead. And since V76 the player plants their own rings on the
   floor of the arena: two objects called mines with opposite meanings, both drawn as a circle at the
   place you are about to tap, is not shippable.

   ---- 2. Enemy fire that follows the pilot ----

   V73's header is the most honest document in this tree and it contains the argument against itself:
   "There is nothing to dodge. Damage does not come from being hit. It comes from anything crossing a
   horizontal line." The fix was to aim every shot at the pilot and then clamp the shooters' timers,
   which raised enemy fire from 2.7 a minute to between 8.7 and 14.7 — **manufacturing a threat to
   justify a subsystem.**

   The pilot keeps walking to your last tap. It is charming, it is free, it makes where you tap mean
   two things, and V83 has since given the arena a real source of pressure that does not need to be
   invented. What goes is the homing and the clamp. What stays, deliberately against the plan, is the
   landing test: a shot that arrives away from the pilot still hits plating for nothing, which is the
   one line that makes the pilot's position matter at all, and it costs nothing to keep.

   ---- 3. V74's impact markers ----

   They exist to read the homing this part just removed, and they draw a second chevron vocabulary
   across the bottom of the arena — which since V76 and V83 is where the player's wells, their
   ammunition and their pilot all live. The one part of the screen that has become busy is the one
   part V74 wanted for itself.

   ---- 4. Three of the four wave directives ----

   PERFECT HUNT and CHAIN REACTION are a scoring system layered on a wave that advances on kills
   regardless of them, competing for attention with the pool, the multiplier, the strike band, the
   cascade bonus and the perfect bonus. Neither changes what the player should aim at.

   NO BREACH does — it revalues V22's shard runner — so it stays, and every non-titan wave now runs
   it. One standing rule the player learns once beats three rotating ones nobody finishes reading,
   and it is the same rule V82's pool now states in points: what gets through costs you. */

const V86={on:true};

if(V86.on){

 /* Mines: the roll itself, so nothing downstream needs to know. V71's marking, its safe-clear
    bonus, its heat charge and V67's red chevron all still work and simply never fire. */
 if(typeof V71==='object')V71.chance=()=>0;

 /* Enemy fire: the clamp first. `fireCap` is applied as a ceiling on every shooter's re-arm timer
    every frame, so raising it to Infinity restores whatever the base loop rolled — 2.4 to 4.0
    seconds — rather than freezing the timers where the clamp last left them. */
 if(typeof V73==='object')V73.fireCap=Infinity;
 /* And the homing. Left as a live function rather than deleted because V73's update calls it
    unconditionally, and the WeakSet it guards is what stops a shot being re-aimed every frame. */
 if(typeof v73Track==='function')v73Track=function(){};

 /* V74: `lead` is the horizon past which a shot is not yet worth marking, tested as
    `if(eta>V74.lead)continue`. At zero every shot fails the test and the pass draws nothing, which
    turns the part off through its own vocabulary instead of through a flag bolted onto it. */
 if(typeof V74==='object')V74.lead=0;

 /* Directives: the rotation is indexed on wave%4 and slot 0 is the titan wave, which is a marker
    rather than a directive and stays. The other three become the one that changes a decision. */
 if(typeof V24_DIRECTIVES!=='undefined'&&V24_DIRECTIVES.length>=4){
  const breach=V24_DIRECTIVES[3];
  V24_DIRECTIVES[1]=breach;
  V24_DIRECTIVES[2]=breach;
 }
}
